+    gates = [value for key, value in report.items() if key.startswith("G")]
+    report["all_passed"] = all(gate.get("pass") is True for gate in gates)
+    return report
+
+
+if __name__ == "__main__":
+    print(json.dumps(selftest(), indent=2, sort_keys=True))

codex
The remaining weakness is intrinsic to a plain planted Erdős–Rényi clique: once a restart happens to enter the plant, every planted neighbor survives. I’m replacing the random core with a randomly relabelled \(k\)-partite compatibility graph—still an ordinary undirected graph under the paper’s definition—with one planted transversal and many statistically similar dead-end partial transversals. This preserves exact inverse generation and makes random greedy completion genuinely exponential in the number of parts.
