@@ -0,0 +1,130 @@
+# Verified problem generator for arXiv:2004.10596
+
+| Profile | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite discrete |
+| Computational core | graph |
+| Certificate form | integer tuple |
+| Native objects | undirected simple graph, hexadecimal adjacency rows, a set of `k` vertices |
+| Intended intuition | invariant — a repeated low-degree neighborhood identifies adjacency rows whose GF(2) parity is the clique indicator |
+| Domain essentiality | native; no reduction |
+
+## What the family asks
+
+The solver receives an undirected simple graph and must return exactly `k` distinct, increasing vertex numbers whose every pair is an edge. This is the native `k`-clique search problem defined in Section 2.6.1 and implemented by the edge and clique detector blocks in Sections 4.1.4–4.1.5 of [*Circuit Design for Clique Problem and Its Implementation on Quantum Computer*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.10596). The graph is printed compactly as fixed-width hexadecimal adjacency rows; the statement defines the bit order completely. Verification is just range, shape, and exact pairwise adjacency testing.
+
+Generation is inverse. It first chooses one vertex in each hidden part, forces those vertices to form a clique, samples the remaining compatibility edges, installs a graph-internal parity probe, and finally applies a random vertex permutation while carrying the known clique through it. It never searches the completed graph. The certificate is JSON-native.
+
+## Why Track B, not Track A
+
+The paper does not prove that a planted random-graph distribution is hard. Claiming Track A from general CLIQUE hardness would therefore be invalid. This family instead exposes a no-tool compression gap grounded in the paper's own algorithm: Section 4.2 explicitly builds all `C(n,k)` vertex combinations, Section 5 gives `O(k C(n,k)+k)` oracle-synthesis cost, and Section 7 gives `O(sqrt(C(n,k)/m))` Grover iterations for `m` marked cliques.
+
+At the shipping setting (`n=216`, `k=8`), the paper-style core search has 103,073,959,989,495 subsets and about 10,152,534 Grover iterations if there is one marked answer. A much better exact classical bitset branch-and-bound solver exists and is disclosed: it has worst-case `O(C(N,k) k^2)` work and measured 2,377 recursive nodes and 0.00642 seconds on average over eight shipping instances. This is precisely why the claim is Track B. After noticing the invariant, the compact route identifies four equal-neighborhood witnesses, reads their common eight-vertex neighborhood, and XORs those eight adjacency rows over GF(2); the measured accounting is 268 exact 64-bit-word operations.
+
+The generated regime avoids two easy cases. A plain planted Erdős–Rényi clique was rejected during development because random greedy restarts solved every small shipping candidate. The final core is a randomly relabelled `k`-partite compatibility graph with a degree-balanced planted transversal and many dead-end partial transversals. It also keeps `k` nonconstant in the family definition even though the shipping ladder holds it at eight to keep the answer writable.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+For `make_instance(seed=3, **DIFFICULTY["demo"])`, the full rendered graph is:
+
+```text
+Find a k-clique in an undirected simple graph. Here k = 4 and N = 18.
+Each hexadecimal row uses bit j for adjacency to vertex j; bit 0 is rightmost.
+
+row degree adjacency_hex
+ 0    3   09002
+ 1    1   00001
+ 2    8   36f00
+ 3    3   01120
+ 4    4   04a40
+ 5    4   01308
+ 6    8   32d90
+ 7    3   08840
+ 8    8   18a6c
+ 9    6   18134
+10    4   02844
+11    9   295d4
+12    5   02829
+13    7   35444
+14    4   0a014
+15    7   14b81
+16    6   0a344
+17    4   02844
+```
+
+Rows 10 and 17 repeat, so their neighborhood is `{2,6,11,13}`. XORing rows 2, 6, 11, and 13 gives bits `{8,9,15,16}`. Thus `<answer>[8,9,15,16]</answer>` verifies as `(True, "ok")`. Dropping 16 gives `(False, "expected exactly 4 vertices")`. A person can solve this demo on paper with four five-digit hexadecimal XORs; exhaustive enumeration finds two valid 4-cliques.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| Preset | Core `n` | `k` | Markers | Witnesses | Status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 12 | 4 | 4 | 2 | hand-solvable illustration |
+| easy | 216 | 8 | 8 | 4 | **provisional shipping preset** |
+| medium | 224 | 8 | 8 | 4 | larger fixed-length haystack |
+| hard | 240 | 8 | 8 | 4 | largest named rung under the 300-operation route cap |
+
+The preset is provisional only because the required external oracle service was unavailable; no local gate rejected it.
+
+## Local gate results
+
+| Gate | Result | Measured evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 planted verifies | pass | 12/12 preset/seed instances; parity construction also recovered every answer |
+| G2 corruptions | pass | empty, wrong length, swapped order, duplicate, and out-of-range answers all rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 round trip | pass | tagged JSON recovered from fenced prose |
+| G4 guess resistance | pass | 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses; bounded candidate space 159,966,582,803,820 |
+| G5 density and cost | pass | shipping density estimate 0/200,000; strongest failing attack used 16,634,880 operation-budget units and 4.763981 s total |
+| G6 adversary panel | pass | all five attacks 0/8; exact reference solver 8/8 |
+| G7 scaling | pass | doubling the core preserves the eight-element answer and enlarges the candidate space |
+| G8 canonical key | pass | 60/60 relabellings invariant, 60/60 carried witnesses valid, 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) caps | pass | 27 characters, about 7 tokens, 8 atoms, 268 intended operations |
+
+The five failing attacks were high-degree outliers, maximum-common-degree greedy search, 16 random greedy restarts, a parity-blind marker common-neighbor ansatz, and 40-step centered spectral power iteration. The successful exact branch-and-bound solver is correctly reported under `reference_algorithm`, not counted as a failing Track B attack.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 diagnostics
+
+The required harness was invoked, but OpenRouter rejected every draw with HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)`. Per the protocol, API errors are not model failures and no hardness verdict can be claimed.
+
+| Preset | Seed | Model | Result | Why |
+|---|---:|---|---|---|
+| easy | 1998662769 | x-ai/grok-4.6 | error | OpenRouter key total limit |
+| easy | 1066944504 | anthropic/claude-sonnet-5 | error | OpenRouter key total limit |
+| easy | 25767657 | anthropic/claude-sonnet-5 | error | OpenRouter key total limit |
+| easy | 621751995 | x-ai/grok-4.6 | error | OpenRouter key total limit |
+
+| G9 arm | Solved / attempts | Status |
+|---|---:|---|
+| bare | 0 / 0 valid calls | blocked by API quota |
+| structural hint | 0 / 0 valid calls | not run |
+| placebo hint | 0 / 0 valid calls | not run |
+
+`hinted - placebo` is therefore not measurable yet. The local size/effort gate is unaffected and passes. The script-owned error transcript is retained rather than replaced with invented evidence. Once quota is restored, rerun the bare arm in this directory and the hinted/placebo arms in separate scratch directories, then replace this table with their actual results.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import importlib.util
+
+spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("clique_gen", "results/2004.10596/gen_2004_10596.py")
+mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
+spec.loader.exec_module(mod)
+
+inst = mod.make_instance(seed=7, **mod.DIFFICULTY[mod.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY])
+question = mod.render(inst)
+answer = mod.parse_answer(f"<answer>{inst['answer']}</answer>")
+assert mod.verify(inst, answer) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+From the repository root, emit twenty deterministic-per-record shipping instances with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2004.10596 20 easy
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+This is not evidence for average-case planted-clique hardness. It is a Track B benchmark whose exact branch solver is fast with tools. The 0/200,000 guess result estimates only the declared prior—uniform increasing `k`-subsets after the obvious degree filter—and is an upper-resolution observation, not proof that the valid-answer density is zero. The demo has two answers, and the verifier intentionally accepts either.
+
+The attack panel does not include SDP relaxations, a production maximum-clique package, or more than 16 randomized greedy restarts. Preliminary high-restart experiments showed that hundreds of tool-driven restarts can sometimes recover the planted clique; that is compatible with Track B but would disqualify a Track A claim. The graph's twin gadget also makes the family easy as soon as its GF(2) role is recognized, which is the intended compression insight. Finally, the four-vendor no-tool evidence is still missing because the supplied OpenRouter key was exhausted; the family must not be submitted as hardened until valid bare, hinted, and placebo transcripts exist.
