 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Verified problem generator for arXiv:2004.10596
     2	
     3	| Profile | Value |
     4	|---|---|
     5	| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
     6	| Native domain | combinatorics |
     7	| Object regime | finite discrete |
     8	| Computational core | graph |
     9	| Certificate form | integer tuple |
    10	| Native objects | undirected simple graph, hexadecimal adjacency rows, a set of `k` vertices |
    11	| Intended intuition | invariant — a repeated low-degree neighborhood identifies adjacency rows whose GF(2) parity is the clique indicator |
    12	| Domain essentiality | native; no reduction |
    13	
    14	## What the family asks
    15	
    16	The solver receives an undirected simple graph and must return exactly `k` distinct, increasing vertex numbers whose every pair is an edge. This is the native `k`-clique search problem defined in Section 2.6.1 and implemented by the edge and clique detector blocks in Sections 4.1.4–4.1.5 of [*Circuit Design for Clique Problem and Its Implementation on Quantum Computer*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.10596). The graph is printed compactly as fixed-width hexadecimal adjacency rows; the statement defines the bit order completely. Verification is just range, shape, and exact pairwise adjacency testing.
    17	
    18	Generation is inverse. It first chooses one vertex in each hidden part, forces those vertices to form a clique, samples the remaining compatibility edges, installs a graph-internal parity probe, and finally applies a random vertex permutation while carrying the known clique through it. It never searches the completed graph. The certificate is JSON-native.
    19	
    20	## Why Track B, not Track A
    21	
    22	The paper does not prove that a planted random-graph distribution is hard. Claiming Track A from general CLIQUE hardness would therefore be invalid. This family instead exposes a no-tool compression gap grounded in the paper's own algorithm: Section 4.2 explicitly builds all `C(n,k)` vertex combinations, Section 5 gives `O(k C(n,k)+k)` oracle-synthesis cost, and Section 7 gives `O(sqrt(C(n,k)/m))` Grover iterations for `m` marked cliques.
    23	
    24	At the shipping setting (`n=216`, `k=8`), the paper-style core search has 103,073,959,989,495 subsets and about 10,152,534 Grover iterations if there is one marked answer. A much better exact classical bitset branch-and-bound solver exists and is disclosed: it has worst-case `O(C(N,k) k^2)` work and measured 2,377 recursive nodes and 0.006677 seconds on average over eight shipping instances. This is precisely why the claim is Track B. After noticing the invariant, the compact route identifies four equal-neighborhood witnesses, reads their common eight-vertex neighborhood, and XORs those eight adjacency rows over GF(2); the measured accounting is 40 exact 64-bit-word operations after recognition (four witness checks, 28 word XORs, and eight set-bit extractions).
    25	
    26	The generated regime avoids two easy cases. A plain planted Erdős–Rényi clique was rejected during development because random greedy restarts solved every small shipping candidate. The final core is a randomly relabelled `k`-partite compatibility graph with a degree-balanced planted transversal and many dead-end partial transversals. It also keeps `k` nonconstant in the family definition even though the shipping ladder holds it at eight to keep the answer writable.
    27	
    28	## Worked demo
    29	
    30	For `make_instance(seed=3, **DIFFICULTY["demo"])`, the full rendered graph is:
    31	
    32	```text
    33	Find a k-clique in an undirected simple graph. Here k = 4 and N = 18.
    34	Each hexadecimal row uses bit j for adjacency to vertex j; bit 0 is rightmost.
    35	
    36	row degree adjacency_hex
    37	 0    3   09002
    38	 1    1   00001
    39	 2    8   36f00
    40	 3    3   01120
    41	 4    4   04a40
    42	 5    4   01308
    43	 6    8   32d90
    44	 7    3   08840
    45	 8    8   18a6c
    46	 9    6   18134
    47	10    4   02844
    48	11    9   295d4
    49	12    5   02829
    50	13    7   35444
    51	14    4   0a014
    52	15    7   14b81
    53	16    6   0a344
    54	17    4   02844
    55	```
    56	
    57	Rows 10 and 17 repeat, so their neighborhood is `{2,6,11,13}`. XORing rows 2, 6, 11, and 13 gives bits `{8,9,15,16}`. Thus `<answer>[8,9,15,16]</answer>` verifies as `(True, "ok")`. Dropping 16 gives `(False, "expected exactly 4 vertices")`. A person can solve this demo on paper with four five-digit hexadecimal XORs; exhaustive enumeration finds two valid 4-cliques.
    58	
