 The paper does not prove that a planted random-graph distribution is hard. Claiming Track A from general CLIQUE hardness would therefore be invalid. This family instead exposes a no-tool compression gap grounded in the paper's own algorithm: Section 4.2 explicitly builds all `C(n,k)` vertex combinations, Section 5 gives `O(k C(n,k)+k)` oracle-synthesis cost, and Section 7 gives `O(sqrt(C(n,k)/m))` Grover iterations for `m` marked cliques.
 
-At the shipping setting (`n=216`, `k=8`), the paper-style core search has 103,073,959,989,495 subsets and about 10,152,534 Grover iterations if there is one marked answer. A much better exact classical bitset branch-and-bound solver exists and is disclosed: it has worst-case `O(C(N,k) k^2)` work and measured 2,377 recursive nodes and 0.006677 seconds on average over eight shipping instances. This is precisely why the claim is Track B. After noticing the invariant, the compact route identifies four equal-neighborhood witnesses, reads their common eight-vertex neighborhood, and XORs those eight adjacency rows over GF(2); the measured accounting is 40 exact 64-bit-word operations after recognition (four witness checks, 28 word XORs, and eight set-bit extractions).
+At the shipping setting (216 core vertices, 12 gadget vertices, hence `N=228` and `k=8`), the paper-style search has 159,966,582,803,820 subsets and about 12,647,789 Grover iterations if there is one marked answer. A much better exact classical bitset branch-and-bound solver exists and is disclosed: for fixed `k` it is `O(N^k)` (`O(N^8)` here), and it measured 2,377 recursive bitset intersections and 0.001–0.003 seconds per instance across local runs. This is precisely why the claim is Track B. After noticing the invariant, the compact route identifies four equal-neighborhood witnesses, reads their common eight-vertex neighborhood, and XORs those eight adjacency rows over GF(2); the measured accounting is 40 exact 64-bit-word operations after recognition (four witness checks, 28 word XORs, and eight set-bit extractions).
 
 The generated regime avoids two easy cases. A plain planted Erdős–Rényi clique was rejected during development because random greedy restarts solved every small shipping candidate. The final core is a randomly relabelled `k`-partite compatibility graph with a degree-balanced planted transversal and many dead-end partial transversals. It also keeps `k` nonconstant in the family definition even though the shipping ladder holds it at eight to keep the answer writable.
 
